, `text`: "Type 2 diabetes symptoms often take several years to develop. Some people don't notice any symptoms at all. Type 2 diabetes usually starts when you're an adult, though more and more children and teens are developing it. Because symptoms are hard to spot, it's important to know the risk factors for type 2 diabetes.\nSome additional symptoms more common in type 2 diabetes include:\nCuts, sores, or wounds that are very slow to heal.\nDark patches of skin around the neck, armpits, or groin.\nNumbness or tingling in the hands or feet.\nSymptoms of gestational diabetes\nGestational diabetes (diabetes during pregnancy) usually doesn't have any symptoms. If you're pregnant, your doctor should test you for gestational diabetes between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy. If needed, you can make changes like healthy eating and being active to protect your health and your baby's health. If those changes aren’t enough to manage your blood sugar, your doctor may prescribe medicine to help.", `title`: "Triệu chứng tiểu đường — nguồn web tự động", `topic`: "Triệu chứng tiểu đường", `url`: "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/signs-symptoms/index.html", `web_source_id`: "cdc-symptoms"};
