ssionals may use the A1C test early in pregnancy to see if a woman with risk factors had undiagnosed diabetes before becoming pregnant. Since the A1C test reflects your average blood glucose levels over the past 3 months, testing early in pregnancy may include values reflecting time before you were pregnant. The glucose challenge test or the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) are used to check for gestational diabetes , usually between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy. If you had gestational diabetes, you should be tested for diabetes no later than 12 weeks after your baby is born. If your blood glucose is still high, you may have type 2 diabetes. Even if your blood glucose is normal, you still have a greater chance of developing type 2 diabetes in the future and should get tested every 3 years.\nCan other blood glucose tests be used to diagnose type 2 diabetes and prediabetes?", `title`: "HbA1c: ý nghĩa, giới hạn và chẩn đoán — nguồn web tự động", `topic`: "HbA1c: ý nghĩa, giới hạn và chẩn đoán", `url`: "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diagnostic-tests/a1c-test", `web_source_id`: "niddk-a1c"};
