`review_status`: "unreviewed_web", `text`: "Seeing your blood glucose levels in real time can help you make more informed decisions about the food and beverages you consume, the physical activity you do, and the medicines you take. Keeping your blood glucose level in your target range can help prevent other health problems caused by diabetes .\nHow does a continuous glucose monitor work?\nA continuous glucose monitor (CGM) estimates what your glucose level is every few minutes and keeps track of it over time.\nA CGM has three parts. First, there is a tiny sensor that can be inserted under your skin, often the skin on your belly or arm, with a sticky patch that helps it stay there. These sensors are called disposable sensors. Another type of CGM sensor—called an implantable sensor—may be placed inside your body. CGM sensors estimate the glucose level in the fluid between your cells, which is very similar to the glucose level in your blood. Sensors must be replaced at specific times, such as every few weeks, depending on the type of sensor you have.", `title`: "Theo dõi glucose liên tục CGM — nguồn web tự động", `topic`: "Theo dõi glucose liên tục CGM", `url`: "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/managing-diabetes/continuous-glucose-monitoring", `web_source_id`: "niddk-cgm"};
