", `text`: "You can view a filtered list of clinical studies on insulin, medicines, and other diabetes treatments covered in this health topic that are federally funded, open, and recruiting at ClinicalTrials.gov . You can expand or narrow the list to include clinical studies from industry, universities, and individuals; however, the National Institutes of Health does not review these studies and cannot ensure they are safe. Always talk with your health care provider before you participate in a clinical study.\n[1] Types of insulin. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Updated March 25, 2021. Accessed January 24, 2022. www.cdc.gov/diabetes/about/how-to-use-insulin.html\n[2] Wong EY, Kroon L. Ultra-rapid-acting insulins: how fast is really needed? Clinical Diabetes . 2021;39(4):415–423. doi:10.2337/cd20-0119\n[3] Collins L, Costello RA. Glucagon-like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists. In: StatPearls [Internet] . StatPearls Publishing; 2021. Updated June 25, 2021. Accessed April 21, 2022. www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551568", `title`: "Insulin và thuốc điều trị đái tháo đường — nguồn web tự động", `topic`: "Insulin và thuốc điều trị đái tháo đường", `url`: "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/insulin-medicines-treatments", `web_source_id`: "niddk-medicines"};
