NIDDK Director Dr. Griffin P. Rodgers explaining the importance of participating in clinical trials.\nWhat clinical studies for pregnancy and diabetes are looking for participants?\nYou can view a filtered list of clinical studies on pregnancy and diabetes that are federally funded, open, and recruiting at ClinicalTrials.gov . You can expand or narrow the list to include clinical studies from industry, universities, and individuals; however, the National Institutes of Health does not review these studies and cannot ensure they are safe for you. Always talk with your primary health care professional before you participate in a clinical study.\n[1] US Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Disease Prevention and Health Promotion. Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans . 2nd ed. US Department of Health and Human Services; 2018. Accessed January 9, 2024. https://health.gov/paguidelines/second-edition/\n[2] American Diabetes Association Professional Practice Committee. 2. Diagnosis and classification of diabetes: standards of care in diabetes—2024. Diabetes Care . 2024;47 (suppl 1):S20–S42. doi:10.2337/dc24-S002", `title`: "Thai kỳ khi đã mắc đái tháo đường — nguồn web tự động", `topic`: "Thai kỳ khi đã mắc đái tháo đường", `url`: "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/diabetes-pregnancy", `web_source_id`: "niddk-pregnancy"};
